    {
      "question_number": 11,
      "question_id": "mathematics_undergraduate_doc_18_mathematics_long_1",
      "question_type": "LONG",
      "stem_text": "In $\\triangle{ABC}$, shown, $\\cos{B}=\\frac{3}{5}$. What is $\\cos{C}$? [asy]\ndraw((0,0)--(4,0)--(0,3)--cycle,black+linewidth(1));\ndraw(rightanglemark((4,0),(0,0),(0,3),10),black+linewidth(1)); label(\"$A$\",(0,0),W);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(0,3),W); label(\"$C$\",(4,0),E); label(\"9\",(0,0)--(0,3),W); [/asy]",
      "options": null,
      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "Since $\\cos{B}=\\frac{3}{5}$, we have $\\cos{B}=\\frac{AB}{BC}=\\frac{9}{BC}=\\frac{3}{5}$.\nThen, we can see that we have $9=BC\\cdot\\frac{3}{5}$, so $BC=9\\cdot\\frac{5}{3}=15$. From\nthe Pythagorean Theorem, we have $AC=\\sqrt{BC^2-AB^2}=\\sqrt{15^2-9^2}=\\sqrt{144}=12$.\nFinally, we can find $\\cos{C}$: $\\cos{C}=\\frac{AC}{BC}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\boxed{\\frac{4}{5}}$.",
      "gold_confidence": 1.0,
      "answer_explanation": "Students should show all steps in their mathematical solution.",
      "source": {
        "dataset": "gsm8k_math",
        "source_id": "12175"
      },
      "has_image": false,
      "image_path": null
    },
