      "question_type": "LONG",
      "stem_text": "What is the value of $b$ if $-x^2+bx-5<0$ only when $x\\in (-\\infty, 1)\\cup(5,\\infty)$?",
      "options": null,
      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "When $x<1$ or $x>5$, $-x^2+bx-5<0$. That means that $-x^2+bx-5=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=5$. So,\nthe parabola has roots at 1 and 5, giving us $(x-1)(x-5)=0$. However, we also know the\nparabola opens downwards since the coefficient of $x^2$ is negative, so we have to negate\none of the factors. We can now write $-x^2+bx-5=(1-x)(x-5)=-x^2+6 x-5$. Thus,\n$b=\\boxed{6}$.",
